amipkg - the AmigaPKG package manager
=====================================

amipkg installs, updates and removes AmigaOS software from the AmigaPKG
repository (a curated, signed catalog of 100+ freely-distributable Amiga
programs from Aminet and elsewhere). It runs on ANY AmigaOS 3.x system - you
do NOT need Amiga Imager.

This bundle already contains the signed catalog, so browsing works OFFLINE
straight after install; `amipkg update` refreshes it online.


Requirements
------------
* AmigaOS 3.0 or newer.
* C:lha         - the LhA unarchiver (standard on almost every system).
* A TCP/IP stack that provides bsdsocket.library, ONLY for downloading
  (Roadshow, AmiTCP, Miami, or an emulator's built-in bsdsocket). Browsing the
  bundled catalog and removing packages need no network. Most downloads use
  plain HTTP - the catalog is Ed25519-signed and verified on-device, and every
  package archive is checked against its SHA-256 pinned in that signed catalog.
* OPTIONAL: AmiSSL 5.x. When installed, amipkg can also download from
  https-only hosts (GitHub releases etc.). Without it, https packages are
  refused with a hint; everything on Aminet works without AmiSSL.
* amipkg itself runs on a 68000; many PACKAGES need 68020+ / more RAM.


Install
-------
1. Make a drawer and extract this archive into it, e.g. from a Shell:
       MakeDir RAM:AmiPKG
       LhA x amipkg.lha RAM:AmiPKG/
2. Run ONE of the two setups (double-click its icon, or Execute it from
   a Shell CD'd into the drawer):

       Install          Self-contained (recommended to try it out):
                        everything stays in this drawer, NOTHING outside
                        it is touched - no Startup-Sequence, no User-
                        Startup. The AMIPKG: assign is re-created by the
                        programs themselves every time they run.
                        Uninstall = delete the drawer.

       Install-System   Integrated: same in-drawer install, PLUS one
                        clearly marked block in S:User-Startup (assign +
                        Shell path), so after a reboot `amipkg` works in
                        every Shell directly. Delete the block to
                        un-integrate.

amipkg LIVES IN THAT DRAWER (like MUI:) - nothing is copied anywhere
else, and NOTHING is written to your Startup-Sequence or User-Startup.
The AMIPKG: assign points at the drawer; catalog, cache and receipt DB
sit next to the binaries. Every amipkg program sets the assign up
itself on every start - even if you skip Install and just launch a
binary, it works. (Shell tip with plain Install: CD into the
drawer first - or use Install-System, which adds the path for you.)


Using it - command line
-----------------------
    amipkg avail             list every package in the catalog
    amipkg avail <term>      search by id / name / category
    amipkg info <id>         show details for one package
    amipkg update            fetch the latest signed catalog (needs network)
    amipkg install <id>      download + SHA-256-verify + install, WITH its
                             dependencies (resolved for your CPU, deps first)
    amipkg adopt <id> <drawer> [<ver>]
                             you ALREADY have this app somewhere? Point amipkg
                             at its drawer: it inventories the files and takes
                             over updates/removal - upgrades land IN PLACE
    amipkg list              list what you have installed
    amipkg check             show which installed packages have updates
    amipkg upgrade [<id>]    upgrade everything out of date (or just <id>);
                             `amipkg upgrade amipkg` self-updates amipkg
    amipkg remove <id>       uninstall (removes the files it installed)
    amipkg dir [<path>]      show/set where recipe-less packages install
                             (default SYS:Programs)

Commands are case-sensitive (install, not Install). Installs check free disk
space first and refuse cleanly when the volume is too full.


Using it - GUI
--------------
TWO GUIs are installed - pick your flavour:

  amipkg-mui   The polished MUI front-end (needs MUI 3.8+ -
               `amipkg install mui38` bootstraps it): resizable
               window, real list columns, description pane.
  amipkg-gui   The GadTools fallback - runs on ANY stock 3.x
               system with zero dependencies.

Both offer the same features. GadTools GUI: toggle Installed / Available (top right),
narrow the list with the Find box, the CATEGORY filter, and the By Name /
Newest sort. Buttons: Update Catalog / Check Updates / Update All / Info /
Install / Run (launch an installed program) / Remove / Refresh. Installs run
in the background - watch the progress line at the bottom; the window stays
usable. Set the install drawer from the Package menu.


How trust works
---------------
The catalog (packages.json) is signed with an offline Ed25519 key; amipkg
verifies that signature on-device before trusting it, so the download host is
untrusted. Every package archive is then verified against the SHA-256 pinned in
the signed catalog. A tampered catalog or archive is refused.


Uninstall amipkg
----------------
    Delete the drawer you unpacked amipkg into. That is ALL - amipkg
    never touches any system file. (If the Shell says the drawer is in
    use: `Assign AMIPKG: REMOVE` first - the session assign holds a lock.)


